<%= UiComponents::Lookbook::TableOfContents.call(page) %>

A revenue overview card: the period's total with a trend badge against the previous period, top markets, a trend
chart and key stats. A **block** composed from Badge, Icon and Sparkline, as on the
[Appica homepage](https://appica.dev/ui).

<%= embed Blocks::RevenueSummaryPreview, :default %>

## Usage

```ruby
RevenueSummary(value: 48_210, previous: 42_900, period: :month) do
  RevenueMarkets do
    @top_markets.each { |market| RevenueMarket(country: market.country_code, value: market.revenue, share: market.share) }
  end
  RevenueChart(data: @monthly_totals, labels: I18n.t("date.abbr_month_names").compact)
  RevenueStats do
    RevenueStat(label: :orders, value: @orders_count)
    RevenueStat(label: :average_order, value: @average_order, money: true)
  end
end
```

- The badge is computed from `value:` and `previous:` - green and rising, or red and falling.
- Every amount (the total, markets and chart tooltips) is formatted with the root's `currency:`.
- The parts are optional and stack in order. The chart pins itself to the bottom of the card when the card is taller
  than its content.
- Stats are equal columns, as many as you pass.

## Localization

The card renders in the current `I18n.locale`. Switch the **Locale** display option in the preview toolbar
(⚙ - English, Čeština, Deutsch, العربية) to try it.

- The engine ships translations under `ui_components.revenue_summary.*` and `ui_components.countries.*`
  (`config/locales/ui_components.{en,cs,de,ar}.yml`). Add a locale by adding the same keys to your app's
  locale files; override any single key the same way.
- `period:` (`:day`, `:week`, `:month`, `:year`) picks the translated title ("Monthly revenue") and the
  comparison ("vs $42,900 last month"). `title:` and `comparison:` take your own wording instead.
- Market names come from the country code (`country: :us` → "United States" / "Spojené státy"); `name:`
  overrides. Stat labels given as symbols (`:orders`, `:average_order`, `:refunds`, `:conversion`) are
  translated; strings are shown as-is.
- Money, counts and percentages use the locale's number formats (delimiters, separators, where the currency
  unit goes), so the host app needs number formats for its locales - e.g. the
  [rails-i18n](https://github.com/svenfuchs/rails-i18n) gem. Chart tooltips follow the same locale.

